# Lab 1 — Fiber Optic Connector Cleaning & Visual Fault Location (VFL) Practice Lab

**Log Type:** `[Real-time]` | Practice lab (bend fault introduced deliberately)  
**Date:** October 2026  
**System / Hardware Profile:** OS2 Single-Mode Fiber (Yellow, 9/125µm), LC-to-LC Duplex Patch Cord, LC Couplers  
**Test Equipment:** BoltBlaze Pen-Style Visual Fault Locator (VFL with FC-Male to LC-Female Adapter), 1.25mm One-Click Cleaner Pen  
**Category:** Fiber Cabling / Physical Layer Diagnostics / Preventive Maintenance  

---

## 1. Objective & Purpose
Perform hands-on connector end-face cleaning, continuity testing, and macrobend fault detection on an OS2 single-mode LC patch cord to practice data center fiber maintenance procedures.

## 2. Safety & Laser Precautions
* **Laser Safety:** Confirmed the patch cord was completely disconnected from active transceivers/network equipment prior to testing.
* **Eye Safety Protocol:** Never pointed the VFL's visible red laser toward anyone's eyes, and never looked into the VFL output or an exposed connector end.

## 3. Connector Cleaning Procedure
1. **Connector in Coupler:** With the patch cord's LC connector seated on one side of the LC coupler, inserted the 1.25mm one-click cleaning pen into the open side and pushed until a tactile "click" was engaged, cleaning the seated connector's end-face.
2. **Bare Connector End-Face:** Removed the coupler and performed one-click end-face cleaning directly on the bare LC connector ferrule.
3. **Limitation:** End-faces were not inspected under a fiber scope, so cleanliness was not verified. Next step: add a fiber inspection scope to follow the inspect-clean-inspect process.

## 4. Visual Fault Location (VFL) Diagnostics
1. **Continuity Verification:** Connected the BoltBlaze VFL (utilizing the FC-to-LC adapter) to one end of the OS2 patch cord and activated continuous red laser output. Verified bright red light emission at the far end connector, confirming end-to-end core continuity.
2. **Macrobend / Fault Isolation Test:** Deliberately introduced a tight bend radius along the fiber run. Observed visible red light leaking through the yellow jacket at the bend, approximately 25% along the cable length from the source end.

## 5. Technical Observations
* **Light Leakage Behavior:** The leak occurred because the bend exceeded the fiber's minimum bend radius (macrobending), causing light to escape the core at that point.
* **Diagnostic Value:** Shows how a VFL can pinpoint tight bends, breaks, and bad connectors on short runs without specialized OTDR equipment. A VFL does not measure loss; that requires a power meter and light source or an OTDR.

## 6. Verification & Final Results
* Confirmed the single-mode fiber core transmitted light end-to-end without complete breaks.
* Re-straightened the fiber loop; light leakage ceased immediately.

## 7. Prevention & Best Practices
* **Bend Radius Rules:** Avoid tight loops or exceeding the manufacturer's minimum bend radius when routing fiber inside rack wire managers.
* **Cleaning Rule:** Always clean connectors before mating to prevent dust-related insertion loss or permanent end-face scratches, and inspect with a scope when one is available.

